{
  // conductor-site - a field guide to agentic engineering.
  // Spec / planDoc: docs/SPEC.md. Tracker: TRACKER.md. Appendix B of the spec is the trap list.
  //
  // TRAPS THIS FILE ITSELF CARRIES:
  //  * `conductor plan set` round-trips this file through System.Text.Json and DROPS EVERY COMMENT,
  //    and the plan EDITOR re-serialises the whole file - silently changing progress.kind and gate
  //    timeouts. Keep this annotated copy safe and `git diff` structurally before any reload.
  //  * `agent.model` is dropped silently unless {model} appears in BOTH args and resumeArgs. It does.
  //    Verify with `conductor journey` - the Model column must never read (default).
  //  * NO `telegram` BLOCK ON PURPOSE. This repo is PUBLIC, and a telegram block in this fleet
  //    carries the owner's chat id. If push notification is wanted, add it in an untracked
  //    local copy - never here.
  //  * NO `templatesDir` ON PURPOSE. The built-in session/fix templates are used, so this run
  //    cannot hit the brace-landmine class: an unresolved {token} in a template file throws and
  //    parks the run. Everything site-specific rides in promptExtra and readOrder instead.
  //  * The `evidence` gate is scoped to S4+ because the script it runs is built during S3.
  //    S3.4 proves it goes red when stale; from S4 it is enforced on every session.
  //  * `defaultWorkflow` IS SET ON PURPOSE. Leaving it unset defaults to `deliver-verify`, which
  //    spawns a separate paid Verify agent session after EVERY deliver - roughly doubling the
  //    session count for a re-audit the engine's own verdict already performs. The `deliver`
  //    workflow below is the one both proven site runs in this fleet used.
  "version": "1.0",
  "planVersion": 1,
  "name": "conductor-site - a field guide to agentic engineering",
  "repo": "C:/code/conductor-site",
  "satelliteRepos": [],
  "tracker": "TRACKER.md",
  "planDoc": "docs/SPEC.md",
  "branchPattern": "^(main|s\\d+/.+)$",
  "pauseOnBlocked": true,

  "agent": {
    "command": "claude",
    "args": [
      "-p", "{prompt}",
      "--output-format", "stream-json",
      "--verbose",
      "--dangerously-skip-permissions",
      "--model", "{model}",
      "--session-id", "{sessionId}"
    ],
    "resumeArgs": [
      "-p", "{prompt}",
      "--output-format", "stream-json",
      "--verbose",
      "--dangerously-skip-permissions",
      "--model", "{model}",
      "--resume", "{claudeSessionId}"
    ],
    "output": "stream-json",
    "model": "claude-opus-5[1m]"
  },

  "advisor": {
    "enabled": true,
    "command": "claude",
    "args": ["-p", "{prompt}", "--output-format", "json", "--model", "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001"],
    "output": "json",
    "timeoutMinutes": 6
  },

  "progress": { "kind": "markdown-table" },

  "stages": [
    {
      "id": "S1", "name": "The face",
      "notes": "docs/SPEC.md Part II is the whole brief. The sixteen colour roles come from C:/code/conductor/face-go/internal/widgets/style.go - the `themes` map, mocha at :58 and latte at :84 - for BOTH schemes, never from the Catppuccin website, because the shipped latte is darkened in-hue to clear a contrast bar stock latte fails. The bar itself is theme_test.go:44-86. Name the role in every rule; a hardcoded hex is right in one scheme and wrong in the other. Body text is a humanist sans at a 62-72ch measure; monospace is reserved for machine truth so the typeface itself carries meaning. S1.4 deletes the two checkPlaceholders allow lines the template ships."
    },
    {
      "id": "S2", "name": "The content model", "dependsOn": ["S1"],
      "notes": "docs/SPEC.md Part III. Three collections - concepts, articles, reports. Schemas import Zod ONLY and nothing Astro-shaped, because api/content.ts imports them outside the build where astro:content does not exist; the template's own schema.ts explains this at length, so read it before editing. Adding a collection touches three places: the schema, the loader in content.config.ts, and the editable map. Miss the third and it silently is not editable. The concept schema carries evidence as KEYS, never values - that is the mechanism behind the whole site's first rule."
    },
    {
      "id": "S3", "name": "The harvest", "dependsOn": ["S2"],
      "notes": "docs/SPEC.md Part VI. Run-level truth comes from `conductor history --json --limit 0`; per-run detail comes from read-only SQLite (mode=ro) against each runDb for costs by category, gate pass rates, bugs, scores, event type counts and rolled-over sessions. Checkpoint counts MUST come from the JSON: a naive CheckpointConfirmed event count answers 65 where the engine answers 287 of 300, measured during planning. anonymise.json fails closed. S3.4 must prove the evidence gate red BOTH ways - stale corpus, and a cited key that is missing."
    },
    {
      "id": "S4", "name": "The concept spine", "dependsOn": ["S3"],
      "notes": "docs/SPEC.md Part IV lists the ten concepts and what each cites. Every page makes the same five moves: the idea (true anywhere, no Conductor), the problem, the mechanism in Conductor with file:line citations, the evidence keys, and something the reader can run. Apply the three litmus tests in Part I to each page before claiming it - especially the third: delete the Conductor section and what is left must still be worth reading. Verify each file:line citation actually resolves in C:/code/conductor before committing it."
    },
    {
      "id": "S5", "name": "The articles", "dependsOn": ["S4"],
      "notes": "docs/SPEC.md Part V names the four and what each is about. Medium length, 1200-2000 words, concise and insight-dense - no filler, no throat-clearing, no enthusiasm as content. Each must carry at least one number nobody else publishes, and every figure is an evidence key rather than a literal. The voice rules in Part I are binding: the banned-words list is not a style preference, it is what separates this from every other page on the topic."
    },
    {
      "id": "S6", "name": "The reports", "dependsOn": ["S5"],
      "notes": "docs/SPEC.md Part VI. Reports are generalised into SCENARIOS the reader can map onto their own situation - that is the point, not only the privacy rule. No client names, no private repo names in prose, no field-note prose quoted. If swapping the real name back in is the only difference, generalise harder. Report B ending at 45 of 46 is deliberately the one we publish, with the shortfall as the subject. The /runs page must name the three abandoned July runs as abandoned, not in-flight."
    },
    {
      "id": "S7", "name": "Ship", "dependsOn": ["S6"], "ownerGated": true,
      "notes": "docs/SPEC.md Part VII. Confirm astro.config site against the real deployed production URL - canonical, hreflang, og:url, og:image, sitemap, robots and the footer credit all derive from that one line. Accessibility and performance in BOTH themes, including no layout shift when the theme flips. Regenerate the three generated artifacts and commit them. S7.4 is owner-gated: the owner reads the three reports for anonymisation and looks at the front page in both themes before this stage confirms."
    }
  ],

  "workflows": {
    "deliver": {
      "name": "deliver",
      "description": "Deliver the next checkpoint; repair only when the battery actually came back red.",
      "steps": [
        { "id": "deliver", "kind": "deliver", "deliver": true },
        { "id": "fix-if-red", "kind": "fix", "runIf": "!gatesGreen", "deliver": true }
      ],
      "repeat": true
    }
  },
  "defaultWorkflow": "deliver",

  "gates": [
    {
      "name": "site-fast",
      "shell": "powershell",
      "command": "Set-Location C:\\code\\conductor-site; npm run check; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { Write-Host 'FAIL: npm run check'; exit 1 }; npm run build; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { Write-Host 'FAIL: npm run build'; exit 1 }; Write-Host 'check + build green'; exit 0",
      "optional": false,
      "tier": "fast",
      "parallel": false,
      "timeoutMinutes": 15
    },
    {
      "name": "generated",
      "shell": "powershell",
      "command": "Set-Location C:\\code\\conductor-site; npm run headers; npm run content; npm run editor; $dirty = git status --porcelain -- vercel.json src/content public/; if ($dirty) { Write-Host 'FAIL: generated files are stale. Regenerate and commit:'; $dirty | ForEach-Object { Write-Host $_ }; exit 1 }; Write-Host 'generated files clean'; exit 0",
      "optional": false,
      "tier": "full",
      "parallel": false,
      "timeoutMinutes": 20
    },
    {
      "name": "evidence",
      "shell": "powershell",
      "command": "Set-Location C:\\code\\conductor-site; node scripts/harvest.mjs --check; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { Write-Host 'FAIL: corpus.json is stale or a cited evidence key is missing'; exit 1 }; Write-Host 'every published figure recomputes from the run store'; exit 0",
      "optional": false,
      "tier": "full",
      "parallel": false,
      "stages": ["S4", "S5", "S6", "S7"],
      "timeoutMinutes": 10
    }
  ],

  "gatePolicy": "stage",
  "verifyEachDelivery": false,

  "limits": {
    "stallMinutes": 15,
    "stallGraceMinutes": 3,
    "sessionTimeoutMinutes": 120,
    "batterySettleSeconds": 120,
    "authPreflight": true,
    "maxResumesPerSession": 2,
    "stageSlackFactor": 2,
    "backoffMinutes": 30,
    "maxBackoffs": 10,
    "maxRunCostUsd": 550,
    "approvalMode": false,
    "maxSessionTokens": 16000000,
    "softBreakRatio": 0.62,
    "maxConcurrentLanes": 1,
    "stallPatternTermination": true,
    "sameFailureCircuitBreaker": true,
    "stallBackoffMinutes": 12,
    "overheadCostPerSecond": 0.0001,
    "verifierThreshold": 80
  },

  "report": { "enabled": true },

  "readOrder": ["docs/SPEC.md", "TRACKER.md", "README.md"],

  "promptExtra": "THIS REPO IS PUBLIC FROM COMMIT ONE. Nothing private goes in - not in content, not in commit messages, not in evidence files, not in the tracker. No client names, no private repo names in report prose, no absolute paths from the owner's machine, no tokens or chat ids. Public repos that MAY be named: conductor, DevContext2, Shamshir, sitekit, site-template. Everything else is described by shape.\n\ndocs/SPEC.md is the authority. Read it before writing anything, and re-read the part that governs your stage. It is complete: Part I is the identity and the three litmus tests, Part II the face, Part III the content model, Part IV the ten concepts, Part V the four articles, Part VI the reports and the anonymisation rule, Appendix A the measured corpus, Appendix B the traps.\n\nEVIDENCE RULES, which this site is about and therefore must obey itself:\n1. No figure is ever typed into content. Content names an evidence KEY; the value comes from src/data/corpus.json, which the harvest recomputes from the run store. A page citing a key that is not in the corpus must fail the build.\n2. Checkpoint counts come from `conductor history --json --limit 0`, NOT from SQL. A naive CheckpointConfirmed count answers 65 where the engine answers 287 of 300. This was measured during planning. Do not re-derive it.\n3. Open every run.db read-only (mode=ro). This machine's store is shared with other repos and with a run that is paused, not finished. Never write to it, never run a conductor verb that mutates another repo's run.\n4. Anonymisation fails closed: a run absent from anonymise.json is excluded from the site, never published under its real name.\n5. ANYTHING BUDGET-SHAPED comes from `conductor budget` and `conductor money`, never from hand-written SQL: floors, median closers, wrap-up, rollover rates, tokens per checkpoint, blended $/M. Those verbs were run in Aug 2026 against a hand-derived analysis of these exact numbers and CONTRADICTED FOUR OF IT - a cap benefit published as 4.0x measured 1.6x, because one window's cost had been divided by another window's checkpoints. Where a hand query and the verbs disagree, the verbs are reading the data.\n6. `runs.limits_json` is NULL for every imported run, so NO CAP VALUE CAN BE RECOMPUTED by the evidence gate. Never publish a cap figure as though the harvest proved it - source it from `conductor budget`, or say on the page that it comes from the plan file and is not gate-verified.\n7. LABEL THE DENOMINATOR on every per-session rate. 340 sessions exist; 315 recorded agent tokens; `conductor budget` divides by costed sessions and says so. Pick one, name it, keep the whole site on it.\n\nCONDUCTOR CITATIONS: the engine source is at C:/code/conductor, checked out on branch feat/karvan. Read it for file:line citations. NEVER commit to it, never run tools/install.ps1, never kill a dotnet or conductor process - a second run may share this machine.\n\nTHE FACE: colour roles come from C:/code/conductor/face-go/internal/widgets/style.go (the themes map), for BOTH schemes. Never from the Catppuccin website - the shipped latte is darkened in-hue because stock latte fails the contrast bar. Name the ROLE in every rule; a hardcoded hex is correct in one scheme and wrong in the other.\n\nASTRO TRAPS ALREADY PAID FOR IN THIS FLEET: the Fonts API hashes family names, so CSS must consume the cssVariable and never the raw name - a token naming the raw family silently renders the fallback forever. The editor stylesheet is COPIED into public/ by `npm run editor`, never imported, because an imported stylesheet changes every page's CSP. `checkAnnotations` fails the build on a data-sk-edit that stops resolving, and `checkPlaceholders` on a TODO or an example.com - both are working as intended, so fix the content rather than the gate.\n\nA stage is not done because the words are written. It is done when `npm run check` is 0 errors, `npm run build` is green, the annotations resolve, and - from S4 - the evidence gate recomputes every figure the stage published."
}
